From 268ebbf31c55682f193291713d3f8d0509902591 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 11:53:26 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] arm: keep a halted core halted when its power domain comes up

On ZynqMP the PMU firmware puts an RPU core to sleep by pulsing its reset
with nCPUHALT asserted, so the core is out of reset but halted. When the
RPU power island is later switched on again (Linux requesting the TCMs
before it loads new firmware), arm_cpu_pwr_cntrl() sets power_state to
PSCI_ON regardless of the halt inputs, the core has work again and
cpu_handle_halt() lets it run: it executes the stale TCM contents while
the PMU firmware clears them and Linux loads the next image.

Take the halt inputs into account when the power input changes, as
arm_cpu_reset_hold() does, and do not leave the halted state while a halt
input is asserted, as is already done for the reset input.
---
 accel/tcg/cpu-exec.c | 3 ++-
 target/arm/cpu.c     | 5 ++++-
 2 files changed, 6 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/accel/tcg/cpu-exec.c b/accel/tcg/cpu-exec.c
index e2231d1..909507f 100644
--- a/accel/tcg/cpu-exec.c
+++ b/accel/tcg/cpu-exec.c
@@ -707,7 +707,8 @@ static inline bool cpu_handle_halt(CPUState *cpu)
                              dev_name, "sleep", 0, 1);
         }
 
-        if (!cpu_has_work(cpu) || cpu->reset_pin) {
+        if (!cpu_has_work(cpu) || cpu->reset_pin || cpu->halt_pin ||
+            cpu->arch_halt_pin) {
             return true;
         }
 
diff --git a/target/arm/cpu.c b/target/arm/cpu.c
index 537cd95..4fcafdd 100644
--- a/target/arm/cpu.c
+++ b/target/arm/cpu.c
@@ -2631,8 +2631,11 @@ static void arm_cpu_pwr_cntrl(void *opaque, int n, int level)
 {
     DeviceClass *dc_parent = DEVICE_CLASS(ARM_CPU_PARENT_CLASS);
     ARMCPU *cpu = ARM_CPU(opaque);
+    CPUState *cs = CPU(opaque);
 
-    cpu->power_state = level ? PSCI_ON : PSCI_OFF;
+    /* Power alone does not start a core that nCPUHALT or halt holds.  */
+    cpu->power_state = level && !cs->halt_pin && !cs->arch_halt_pin ?
+                           PSCI_ON : PSCI_OFF;
     dc_parent->pwr_cntrl(opaque, n, level);
 }
 
